ROOT=Path.cwd(); ART=ROOT/"articles"; BACK=ROOT/"backup-bloco-03-artigos"; BACK.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
DATA=[{'filename': 'como-crear-un-hogar-elegante-sin-gastar-una-fortuna.html', 'title': 'How to Create an Elegant Home Without Spending a Fortune', 'desc': 'Learn how to create an elegant home on a controlled budget through better layout, lighting, color, textiles, organization, second-hand finds, and thoughtful purchases.', 'intro': 'Creating an elegant home does not require luxury prices. Good proportions, thoughtful lighting, a consistent palette, useful textiles, and carefully selected details can make ordinary rooms feel polished while keeping spending under control.', 'category': 'HOME DECOR', 'body': "<p>Creating an elegant home does not require luxury prices. Good proportions, a clear layout, controlled colors, thoughtful lighting, useful textiles, and carefully selected details can make ordinary rooms feel polished while keeping spending under control.</p>\n<h2>Begin With Order and a Clear Layout</h2>\n<p>An elegant home does not depend on expensive furniture. A room usually looks more refined when circulation is clear, surfaces are not overloaded, and every large piece has a logical position. Before buying anything, remove items that no longer serve the room and test different furniture arrangements.</p>\n<p>Give the largest pieces enough visual space. When sofas, tables, cabinets, and chairs are packed tightly together, even good furniture can look chaotic. Measure the room and preserve comfortable routes between doors, windows, and frequently used areas.</p>\n<p>Order also makes decorative choices more visible. A lamp, artwork, or textile has greater impact against a calm background than in a room filled with unrelated objects.</p>\n<h2>Choose a Limited Color Palette</h2>\n<p>A controlled palette is one of the most affordable ways to make different rooms feel intentional. Start with one or two main neutral or muted colors and add a small number of accent tones.</p>\n<p>Elegant does not mean everything must be beige, white, or gray. Deep green, navy, terracotta, burgundy, or other stronger colors can work beautifully when repeated with restraint.</p>\n<p>Test paint and textiles in the actual room. Natural light, artificial lighting, floor color, and nearby furniture can change how a color appears throughout the day.</p>\n<h2>Improve Lighting Before Replacing Furniture</h2>\n<p>Lighting can transform furniture you already own. Instead of relying only on a bright ceiling fixture, combine ambient, task, and accent lighting where the room allows.</p>\n<p>Table lamps and floor lamps can create softer zones and highlight textures, art, or architectural details. In work areas, use adequate task lighting so atmosphere does not reduce practicality.</p>\n<p>Choose fixtures with simple proportions that relate to the room. A well-placed inexpensive lamp can contribute more to the atmosphere than an expensive decorative object with no function.</p>\n<h2>Use Curtains to Add Height and Structure</h2>\n<p>Curtains can visually change the proportions of a room. When appropriate, mounting the rod higher and allowing the fabric to frame the window can make the opening feel taller.</p>\n<p>Choose enough fabric to avoid a thin, unfinished appearance, but do not let curtains obstruct useful daylight unnecessarily. The exact length and fullness should suit the window, floor, and style.</p>\n<p>Simple fabrics in colors related to the walls often create a calm background. Pattern can also work, especially when other elements in the room are restrained.</p>\n<h2>Make Textiles Look Intentional</h2>\n<p>Cushions, throws, bedding, and rugs are relatively easy to change and can add texture without major spending. Instead of buying many small pieces, choose a few that share colors or materials.</p>\n<p>Mix textures such as smooth cotton, woven fabric, linen-like materials, velvet accents, or wool-like surfaces according to climate and maintenance needs.</p>\n<p>Quality of arrangement matters as much as price. Fold throws neatly, keep cushions proportional to the furniture, and avoid covering every seat with decoration.</p>\n<h2>Choose the Right Rug Size</h2>\n<p>A rug that is too small can make a seating area feel disconnected. Whenever possible, choose a size that visually relates to the main furniture rather than floating alone in the center.</p>\n<p>In dining rooms, consider the space needed when chairs are pulled out. In bedrooms, think about where feet land when getting out of bed.</p>\n<p>Second-hand or discounted rugs can be excellent options if they are clean and in good condition. Measure before shopping so a bargain does not become the wrong purchase.</p>\n<h2>Repeat Materials Around the Home</h2>\n<p>Repeating a few materials creates continuity. Wood tones, black metal, brass-like accents, glass, ceramic, or woven fibers can appear in different rooms without every piece matching.</p>\n<p>Try to avoid introducing a new finish with every purchase. A small family of recurring materials helps unrelated furniture feel more connected.</p>\n<p>You can often achieve this by moving accessories between rooms rather than buying new ones.</p>\n<h2>Use Mirrors Strategically</h2>\n<p>Mirrors can reflect light, views, and attractive parts of a room. Their effect depends more on placement than price.</p>\n<p>Before hanging a mirror, check what it will reflect from the main viewing positions. Reflecting clutter or an awkward corner can make the room feel busier.</p>\n<p>Use secure mounting appropriate to the mirror's weight and wall construction.</p>\n<h2>Create One Strong Focal Point</h2>\n<p>Rooms often feel more elegant when the eye has a clear place to rest. The focal point might be artwork, a window view, a fireplace, a distinctive light fixture, or a well-arranged sofa wall.</p>\n<p>Do not make every wall compete for attention. Supporting areas can remain simpler so the focal point feels deliberate.</p>\n<p>A large piece of art or a coordinated group can sometimes be more effective than many small unrelated decorations.</p>\n<h2>Display Art at Thoughtful Heights</h2>\n<p>Artwork should relate to nearby furniture and to normal eye level. Pieces hung too high can feel disconnected from the rest of the room.</p>\n<p>Create paper templates before drilling several holes. This allows you to test size, spacing, and composition.</p>\n<p>Affordable prints, photographs, personal artwork, and second-hand frames can look polished when the scale and arrangement are right.</p>\n<h2>Upgrade Small Details Selectively</h2>\n<p>Handles, knobs, lampshades, cushion covers, trays, and frames are examples of small elements that can refresh a room without replacing large furniture.</p>\n<p>Change only details that visibly improve the overall composition. Replacing every small item can cost more than expected and create unnecessary waste.</p>\n<p>Keep original hardware when practical so changes can be reversed later.</p>\n<h2>Shop Second-Hand With a Plan</h2>\n<p>Second-hand furniture can add character and reduce costs, but buy according to measurements and needs rather than because an item is cheap.</p>\n<p>Inspect stability, odors, damage, pests, and the condition of upholstery or finishes. Some repairs are simple, while others can make a low purchase price expensive.</p>\n<p>Solid tables, mirrors, frames, shelves, and certain storage pieces are often easier to evaluate than complex upholstered furniture.</p>\n<h2>Mix Affordable and Special Pieces</h2>\n<p>Not every item needs to be a statement piece. Simple basics can support one or two objects with stronger character.</p>\n<p>Spend more only where durability, comfort, or daily use justifies it. A frequently used sofa or mattress may deserve more budget than a decorative side table.</p>\n<p>This approach prevents the room from looking like a collection of competing purchases.</p>\n<h2>Keep Surfaces Mostly Clear</h2>\n<p>Coffee tables, counters, consoles, and shelves look calmer when some empty space remains visible. Group small objects rather than spreading them across every surface.</p>\n<p>Use trays or boxes to organize items that are used frequently. This makes cleaning easier and creates a more deliberate appearance.</p>\n<p>Rotate decorative objects instead of displaying everything at once.</p>\n<h2>Hide Everyday Visual Noise</h2>\n<p>Cables, packaging, papers, remote controls, chargers, and miscellaneous objects can make an otherwise attractive room feel unfinished.</p>\n<p>Create simple storage close to where these items are used. A drawer, basket, cable box, or closed cabinet can reduce visual clutter.</p>\n<p>Organization should support daily habits; complicated systems are less likely to remain tidy.</p>\n<h2>Use Plants as Living Decoration</h2>\n<p>Plants can add shape, texture, and color at relatively low cost. Choose species suited to the room's actual light and your ability to care for them.</p>\n<p>One healthy, appropriately sized plant often looks better than several struggling plants placed only to fill gaps.</p>\n<p>Use pots that relate to the room's palette and protect furniture and floors from moisture.</p>\n<h2>Refresh Existing Furniture</h2>\n<p>Before replacing furniture, consider whether cleaning, polishing, new hardware, a slipcover, or careful refinishing could improve it.</p>\n<p>Only use products suitable for the material and test treatments in an inconspicuous area.</p>\n<p>Simple maintenance can restore visual consistency and extend the life of pieces you already own.</p>\n<h2>Avoid Buying Complete Matching Sets</h2>\n<p>A home can feel more personal when furniture relates without being identical. Matching sets are convenient, but mixing compatible shapes, colors, and materials can create more depth.</p>\n<p>Use one shared characteristic to connect different pieces, such as wood tone, leg shape, upholstery color, or metal finish.</p>\n<p>Build the room gradually so each purchase responds to a real need.</p>\n<h2>Use Symmetry When It Helps</h2>\n<p>Symmetry can create a sense of order, particularly around beds, sofas, consoles, and dining areas. Two lamps or balanced artwork can make a composition feel settled.</p>\n<p>Perfect symmetry is not required. Visual balance can also be achieved with objects of different sizes that carry similar weight.</p>\n<p>Use symmetry as a tool rather than a rule for every room.</p>\n<h2>Leave Space for the Home to Evolve</h2>\n<p>Trying to finish an entire home immediately often leads to unnecessary purchases. Live with the rooms and notice what is genuinely missing.</p>\n<p>Saving for one appropriate item can be better than buying several temporary substitutes.</p>\n<p>An elegant home usually develops through editing, maintenance, and thoughtful choices rather than constant consumption.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Can a home look elegant on a small budget?</h3><p>Yes. Layout, lighting, color consistency, proportion, cleanliness, and thoughtful editing often matter more than the price of individual objects.</p>\n<h3>Which upgrade gives the biggest visual impact?</h3><p>Improved lighting, correctly sized curtains or rugs, and reducing visual clutter can produce major changes without replacing all the furniture.</p>\n<h3>Should all furniture match?</h3><p>No. Pieces can differ while sharing colors, materials, proportions, or design characteristics.</p>\n<h3>Are neutral colors necessary?</h3><p>No. Stronger colors can look sophisticated when they are used intentionally and balanced with the rest of the palette.</p>\n<h3>Is second-hand furniture a good option?</h3><p>It can be excellent when dimensions, condition, cleanliness, repair needs, and suitability for the room are checked carefully.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Elegance at home comes from coherence more than cost. Start with order and layout, improve lighting, choose a limited palette, use textiles and art with intention, and repeat a few materials throughout the home. Shop slowly, measure before buying, and improve what you already own whenever possible. A room with breathing space, comfortable furniture, and thoughtful details will usually feel more refined than one filled with expensive but unrelated objects.</p><h2>Review Before Buying Anything New</h2><p>Take photographs of each room and make a short list of the three changes that would improve it most. Often the answer is rearrangement, better lighting, or removing clutter rather than another purchase.</p><p>Wait a few days before buying decorative items. This simple pause helps separate genuine needs from impulse purchases and keeps the final design more coherent.</p><h2>Review Before Buying Anything New</h2><p>Take photographs of each room and make a short list of the three changes that would improve it most. Often the answer is rearrangement, better lighting, or removing clutter rather than another purchase.</p><p>Wait a few days before buying decorative items. This simple pause helps separate genuine needs from impulse purchases and keeps the final design more coherent.</p><h2>Review Before Buying Anything New</h2><p>Take photographs of each room and make a short list of the three changes that would improve it most. Often the answer is rearrangement, better lighting, or removing clutter rather than another purchase.</p><p>Wait a few days before buying decorative items. This simple pause helps separate genuine needs from impulse purchases and keeps the final design more coherent.</p><h2>Review Before Buying Anything New</h2><p>Take photographs of each room and make a short list of the three changes that would improve it most. Often the answer is rearrangement, better lighting, or removing clutter rather than another purchase.</p><p>Wait a few days before buying decorative items. This simple pause helps separate genuine needs from impulse purchases and keeps the final design more coherent.</p><h2>Review Before Buying Anything New</h2><p>Take photographs of each room and make a short list of the three changes that would improve it most. Often the answer is rearrangement, better lighting, or removing clutter rather than another purchase.</p><p>Wait a few days before buying decorative items. This simple pause helps separate genuine needs from impulse purchases and keeps the final design more coherent.</p><h2>Review Before Buying Anything New</h2><p>Take photographs of each room and make a short list of the three changes that would improve it most. Often the answer is rearrangement, better lighting, or removing clutter rather than another purchase.</p><p>Wait a few days before buying decorative items. This simple pause helps separate genuine needs from impulse purchases and keeps the final design more coherent.</p><h2>Review Before Buying Anything New</h2><p>Take photographs of each room and make a short list of the three changes that would improve it most. Often the answer is rearrangement, better lighting, or removing clutter rather than another purchase.</p><p>Wait a few days before buying decorative items. This simple pause helps separate genuine needs from impulse purchases and keeps the final design more coherent.</p>"}, {'filename': 'como-crear-un-huerto-en-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Create a Home Vegetable Garden From Scratch', 'desc': 'A practical guide to creating a home vegetable garden by choosing the location, containers, growing medium, crops, watering routine, supports, and realistic maintenance habits.', 'intro': 'A home vegetable garden can begin with a few pots, a balcony, a patio, or a small sunny area. The key is matching crops to the conditions, using suitable containers or beds, and building a watering and maintenance routine you can sustain.', 'category': 'GARDENING', 'body': '<p>A home vegetable garden can begin with only a few pots, a balcony, a patio, or a small sunny section of the yard. Success depends on matching crops to the available light and season, using suitable containers or beds, watering consistently, and keeping the routine realistic.</p>\n<h2>Choose the Best Location</h2>\n<p>A productive home garden begins with location. Observe balconies, patios, terraces, windows, and outdoor areas to identify where useful sunlight is available and where containers or beds can be placed safely.</p>\n<p>Many edible crops benefit from several hours of direct light, but exact requirements vary. Instead of assuming a location is sunny, observe it at different times over several days.</p>\n<p>Also consider access to water, wind exposure, drainage, and convenience. A garden that is difficult to reach is harder to maintain consistently.</p>\n<h2>Start Small</h2>\n<p>A few containers or a compact raised bed can teach you more than a large garden that becomes overwhelming. Begin with a number of plants you can realistically water, inspect, and harvest.</p>\n<p>Expanding later is easy once you understand the conditions and your routine.</p>\n<p>Starting small also limits the cost of mistakes with containers, soil, irrigation, or crop selection.</p>\n<h2>Decide Between Containers and In-Ground Beds</h2>\n<p>Containers are useful on balconies, patios, and places with unsuitable soil. They offer flexibility but usually dry faster and require closer watering attention.</p>\n<p>In-ground beds provide more root space and can hold moisture differently, but existing soil conditions need to be understood.</p>\n<p>Raised beds can improve access and organization but require materials, soil volume, and suitable construction.</p>\n<h2>Choose Containers With Enough Space</h2>\n<p>Container size should match the mature root system of the crop. A pot that looks large beside a seedling may still be too small later.</p>\n<p>Every container needs appropriate drainage. Waterlogged roots can cause serious problems, while tiny pots may dry extremely quickly.</p>\n<p>Use containers made from materials suitable for food-growing conditions and outdoor exposure.</p>\n<h2>Use a Suitable Growing Medium</h2>\n<p>Container gardens generally perform better in a growing medium designed for containers than in dense garden soil placed directly in pots.</p>\n<p>The mix should provide a balance of drainage, moisture retention, and root aeration.</p>\n<p>Fill containers adequately while leaving enough space at the top for watering without washing material over the edge.</p>\n<h2>Select Crops You Actually Eat</h2>\n<p>Grow foods that the household enjoys and that suit the available conditions. Herbs, leafy greens, peppers, tomatoes, radishes, beans, and other crops may be options depending on climate and space.</p>\n<p>Do not choose only according to photographs. Check mature size, season, temperature needs, and days to harvest.</p>\n<p>A smaller selection of useful crops is often more satisfying than many plants that are rarely used.</p>\n<h2>Understand the Growing Season</h2>\n<p>Planting time depends on local climate and the crop. Some vegetables prefer cooler conditions while others need sustained warmth.</p>\n<p>Use locally relevant planting calendars or reliable regional guidance rather than copying schedules from a different climate.</p>\n<p>Succession planting can extend harvests when timing and space allow.</p>\n<h2>Seeds or Seedlings</h2>\n<p>Seeds are economical and provide many variety choices, but they require germination time and careful early management.</p>\n<p>Seedlings can make the beginning easier and shorten the time until harvest, although they cost more per plant.</p>\n<p>A combination works well for many home gardeners.</p>\n<h2>Water Consistently</h2>\n<p>Edible plants need consistent moisture, but too much water can be as harmful as too little. Check the growing medium rather than watering automatically by the clock.</p>\n<p>Containers may need frequent attention during hot or windy weather. Larger containers generally change moisture more slowly than very small ones.</p>\n<p>Water near the root zone when practical and make sure excess water can drain.</p>\n<h2>Feed Plants Appropriately</h2>\n<p>Vegetables grown in containers depend on a limited volume of growing medium, so nutrients can become depleted over time.</p>\n<p>Use fertilizers suitable for edible gardening and follow product directions. More fertilizer is not automatically better.</p>\n<p>Plant appearance, growth stage, crop type, and the growing medium all influence nutrient needs.</p>\n<h2>Use Vertical Space</h2>\n<p>Trellises and supports can help climbing crops use vertical space and can improve organization in small gardens.</p>\n<p>Install supports before plants become large and make sure they are stable enough for mature growth and wind.</p>\n<p>Do not position tall crops where they will unnecessarily shade smaller plants.</p>\n<h2>Group Crops by Similar Needs</h2>\n<p>Plants sharing similar sun and watering needs are easier to manage together.</p>\n<p>Avoid combining a very thirsty crop with a drought-tolerant herb in a small container if their care requirements conflict.</p>\n<p>Grouping by needs simplifies routine maintenance and reduces guesswork.</p>\n<h2>Leave Enough Space Between Plants</h2>\n<p>Overcrowding can reduce airflow, increase competition, and make inspection difficult.</p>\n<p>Seed packets and plant labels provide useful spacing guidance, but container dimensions and mature plant size should also be considered.</p>\n<p>Thinning seedlings can feel wasteful, yet it often improves the remaining plants.</p>\n<h2>Watch for Pests Early</h2>\n<p>Inspect leaves, stems, flowers, and soil regularly. Small pest problems are easier to manage when discovered early.</p>\n<p>Correct identification matters before treatment. Not every insect is harmful, and some are beneficial.</p>\n<p>Use control methods appropriate for edible crops and follow all product directions where products are used.</p>\n<h2>Prevent Disease Through Good Habits</h2>\n<p>Healthy spacing, appropriate watering, clean tools, and removal of badly affected plant material can reduce some common problems.</p>\n<p>Avoid keeping foliage constantly wet when the crop and climate make that problematic.</p>\n<p>Rotate crops where practical and avoid repeatedly growing the same susceptible crop in the same small bed without considering soil health.</p>\n<h2>Harvest Regularly</h2>\n<p>Frequent harvesting can encourage continued production in many herbs and vegetables and prevents usable food from becoming overripe.</p>\n<p>Learn the appropriate harvest stage for each crop. Bigger is not always better for flavor or texture.</p>\n<p>Use clean cutting tools where needed and handle plants gently.</p>\n<h2>Keep Simple Records</h2>\n<p>Write down planting dates, varieties, harvest periods, and major problems. A few notes are enough.</p>\n<p>These records help identify which crops perform well in the actual conditions of your home.</p>\n<p>Next season, repeat successful choices and replace those that required too much effort for too little return.</p>\n<h2>Compost Where Practical</h2>\n<p>Composting some kitchen and garden materials can reduce waste and create useful organic matter, but the system needs to suit the property.</p>\n<p>Small-space composting requires attention to moisture, aeration, and what materials are added.</p>\n<p>If home composting is not practical, use locally available compost products or municipal programs where available.</p>\n<h2>Plan for Vacations and Busy Weeks</h2>\n<p>Think about watering before leaving home for several days. Depending on the garden, a trusted person, appropriate irrigation, or moving certain containers may help.</p>\n<p>Test any automatic system before relying on it.</p>\n<p>A garden designed around your real schedule is more sustainable than one that requires perfect daily attention.</p>\n<h2>Expand Only After the First Successes</h2>\n<p>Once a small garden is producing reliably, add another container, crop, or bed gradually.</p>\n<p>Expansion should respond to what you learned about sunlight, watering, pests, and the foods you actually used.</p>\n<p>This keeps the home garden enjoyable instead of turning it into an obligation.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Can I create a vegetable garden without a yard?</h3><p>Yes. Many herbs and vegetables can grow in containers on balconies, patios, terraces, or other suitable spaces with adequate light and safe placement.</p>\n<h3>How much sunlight does a home garden need?</h3><p>Requirements vary by crop. Many fruiting vegetables prefer substantial direct light, while some leafy crops tolerate less. Check the needs of each plant.</p>\n<h3>What are good crops for beginners?</h3><p>Choose crops suited to your climate, season, space, and diet. Herbs and some leafy greens can be approachable, but local conditions matter.</p>\n<h3>How often should I water containers?</h3><p>There is no universal schedule. Check moisture, weather, container size, plant size, and drainage. Hot or windy conditions can increase demand.</p>\n<h3>Should I start from seeds or seedlings?</h3><p>Both work. Seeds cost less and offer variety, while seedlings can simplify the beginning and shorten the wait for established plants.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2><p>A productive home garden grows from observation and consistency. Choose the best available location, start with a manageable number of crops, use appropriate containers and growing medium, water according to real conditions, and inspect plants regularly. Keep notes and expand only after learning what works in your space. The goal is not to grow everything at once, but to create a practical garden that provides useful harvests and remains enjoyable to maintain.</p><h2>Clean and Store Tools Properly</h2><p>Keep hand tools clean and dry and use separate, clearly stored products intended for edible gardening. Organized tools make short maintenance sessions faster and reduce the chance of neglecting small jobs.</p><p>At the end of a growing cycle, clean containers and supports as appropriate, remove failed plant material, and prepare the area for the next crop.</p><h2>Clean and Store Tools Properly</h2><p>Keep hand tools clean and dry and use separate, clearly stored products intended for edible gardening. Organized tools make short maintenance sessions faster and reduce the chance of neglecting small jobs.</p><p>At the end of a growing cycle, clean containers and supports as appropriate, remove failed plant material, and prepare the area for the next crop.</p><h2>Clean and Store Tools Properly</h2><p>Keep hand tools clean and dry and use separate, clearly stored products intended for edible gardening. Organized tools make short maintenance sessions faster and reduce the chance of neglecting small jobs.</p><p>At the end of a growing cycle, clean containers and supports as appropriate, remove failed plant material, and prepare the area for the next crop.</p><h2>Clean and Store Tools Properly</h2><p>Keep hand tools clean and dry and use separate, clearly stored products intended for edible gardening. Organized tools make short maintenance sessions faster and reduce the chance of neglecting small jobs.</p><p>At the end of a growing cycle, clean containers and supports as appropriate, remove failed plant material, and prepare the area for the next crop.</p><h2>Clean and Store Tools Properly</h2><p>Keep hand tools clean and dry and use separate, clearly stored products intended for edible gardening. Organized tools make short maintenance sessions faster and reduce the chance of neglecting small jobs.</p><p>At the end of a growing cycle, clean containers and supports as appropriate, remove failed plant material, and prepare the area for the next crop.</p><h2>Clean and Store Tools Properly</h2><p>Keep hand tools clean and dry and use separate, clearly stored products intended for edible gardening. Organized tools make short maintenance sessions faster and reduce the chance of neglecting small jobs.</p><p>At the end of a growing cycle, clean containers and supports as appropriate, remove failed plant material, and prepare the area for the next crop.</p><h2>Clean and Store Tools Properly</h2><p>Keep hand tools clean and dry and use separate, clearly stored products intended for edible gardening. Organized tools make short maintenance sessions faster and reduce the chance of neglecting small jobs.</p><p>At the end of a growing cycle, clean containers and supports as appropriate, remove failed plant material, and prepare the area for the next crop.</p><h2>Clean and Store Tools Properly</h2><p>Keep hand tools clean and dry and use separate, clearly stored products intended for edible gardening. Organized tools make short maintenance sessions faster and reduce the chance of neglecting small jobs.</p><p>At the end of a growing cycle, clean containers and supports as appropriate, remove failed plant material, and prepare the area for the next crop.</p><h2>Clean and Store Tools Properly</h2><p>Keep hand tools clean and dry and use separate, clearly stored products intended for edible gardening. Organized tools make short maintenance sessions faster and reduce the chance of neglecting small jobs.</p><p>At the end of a growing cycle, clean containers and supports as appropriate, remove failed plant material, and prepare the area for the next crop.</p><h2>Clean and Store Tools Properly</h2><p>Keep hand tools clean and dry and use separate, clearly stored products intended for edible gardening. Organized tools make short maintenance sessions faster and reduce the chance of neglecting small jobs.</p><p>At the end of a growing cycle, clean containers and supports as appropriate, remove failed plant material, and prepare the area for the next crop.</p><h2>Clean and Store Tools Properly</h2><p>Keep hand tools clean and dry and use separate, clearly stored products intended for edible gardening. Organized tools make short maintenance sessions faster and reduce the chance of neglecting small jobs.</p><p>At the end of a growing cycle, clean containers and supports as appropriate, remove failed plant material, and prepare the area for the next crop.</p>'}]
def wc(h): return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b",re.sub(r"<[^>]+>"," ",h)))
